 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Training Pipeline Consistency](13141-training-pipeline-consistency.md)
-A strategy to minimize the gap between training and rendering by using the same block-based volume rendering pipeline during training as in the final rendering stage.
+Training Pipeline Consistency is a strategy to minimize the discrepancy between the training and inference phases of block-based volume rendering by enforcing the identical rendering pipeline during both stages. It solves the problem where training with a continuous, global coordinate system leads to artifacts or quality degradation when the model is deployed in a constrained, block-based rendering environment. It sets itself apart from standard neural rendering methods by explicitly simulating the discrete, hardware-bound constraints of the target rendering hardware (such as texture unit limits and quantization) during the optimization process.
 
 ### [Patch-based Volume Rendering](13945-patch-based-volume-rendering.md)
-A memory-saving rendering strategy for high-resolution volumes that splits the spatial dimensions into overlapping patches, renders them independently, and merges the results to scale to arbitrary image resolutions.
+A memory-saving rendering strategy for high-resolution volumes that splits the spatial dimensions into overlapping patches, renders them independently, and merges the results to scale to arbitrary image resolutions. Unlike full-volume rendering which requires memory proportional to $H \times W$, the patch-based approach trades redundant computation in overlapping regions for drastically reduced memory footprint, enabling scaling to arbitrary resolutions.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of blocks $M$ and the number of rays, as each block must be processed sequentially per ray [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced per-shader memory footprint, as each shader only loads resources for one block [S1]. However, total memory usage can still be high if all blocks are loaded simultaneously (e.g., at high altitudes) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of blocks $M$ for the sorting and accumulation steps, plus the cost of rendering each block [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of active blocks loaded into shaders, constrained by the texture unit limit (typically 16) [S1].
 *   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** Supports dynamic loading of resources via Level-of-Detail (LOD) to manage memory on consumer devices [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables rendering of large-scale scenes on web platforms with strict texture unit limits [S1]. Maintains 3D consistency and correct occlusion [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Sequential rendering may introduce overhead compared to unified shader approaches [S1]. Memory usage can exceed consumer device capacity if all blocks are loaded [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering large scenes on web platforms where texture unit limits prevent loading all data into a single shader [S1].
-*   **When not:** When real-time performance is critical and the overhead of sequential shader switching is prohibitive, or when sufficient GPU memory exists to load all blocks into a single shader [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Supports dynamic loading of resources via Level-of-Detail (LOD) generation to handle large scenes on consumer devices [S1].
